  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n\nWe'll use a combination of multi-resolution optimization, gradient-based local search, and global exploration using Fourier features. Unlike current methods that rely primarily on local hill climbing, this approach systematically explores different frequency structures to find functions with low maximum overlap. The core idea is to parametrize the function using a Fourier basis and then optimize the coefficients with projection onto the feasible set (0 <= h(x) <= 1, integral = 1). This leverages the spectral properties of the overlap function while allowing structured exploration of the solution space. We'll use a tempering schedule with exponentially decreasing step sizes, ensuring both exploration and exploitation. A key innovation is the use of a knowledge base storing previously validated function segments, enabling efficient morphing between solutions rather than random generation.\n\nThe algorithm will operate in three phases:\na) Multi-resolution search from coarse to fine grids\nb) Fourier-based optimization with projection\nc) Morph operator between successful solutions\n\nBottlenecks addressed:\n- Existing local searches easily get stuck in poor optima\n- FFT-based methods operate too slowly for high grids\n- Initial random generation lacks direction\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n\nWe represent our function using a cosine basis with amplitude and phase modulation:\n\nh(x) = sum_{k=1}^{K} A_k cos(2\u03c0 f_k x) + b\n\nwhere:\n- x \u2208 [0,2]\n- K: number of Fourier components\n- f_k: frequency parameters\n- A_k: amplitudes\n- b: baseline\n\nIntegral constraint:\n\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n\nWe require h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for all x. The objective is:\n\nC_5(h) = (2/n) \u00d7 max_k [\u221a(\u222b h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx)]   [FOLLOWUP_EDITOR NOTE: Note the square root transformation is not justified in the original problem and should be removed]\n\nThe optimization proceeds in the transformed space with projecting constraints to maintain feasibility.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n\nInitialize:\n- n = 720 (if parent exists) or 360 (fallback)\n- K = min(floor(log\u2082(n))+1, 12)\n- best_global = None\n- knowledge_base = []  # Valid function segments\n\nPhase 1: Low-resolution initialization\n- For resolution_lengths = [360, 720]:\n  - Generate candidate with random frequencies\n  - Project to satisfy integral constraint\n  - Add to knowledge_base if valid\n  - Set best_global = min(current best, candidate)\n\nPhase 2: Multi-resolution optimization\n- For max_iter=800 to max_time remaining:\n  - Select random subset of knowledge_base (size 5)\n  - For each, morph using Fourier interpolation:\n    - Sort by magnitude of coefficients\n    - Linear interpolate between top coefficients\n    - Gradually introduce random mutations\n  - Maintain 8-parameter vector of best Fourier coefficients\n  - Use L-BFGS-B (gradient-based) on projected space:\n    - Gradient computed via automatic differentiation\n    - Bound constraints handled via projection\n\nPhase 3: Down-sampling fallback\n- If plateau detected or grid too large:\n  - Sample random segment from knowledge_base\n  - Down-sample to smaller grid, maintaining shape\n  - Continue optimization\n\nProjection mechanism:\n- After each update, apply projector:\n    h_proj = max(0, min(1, h))\n    h_proj = h_proj - (sum(h_proj) - n/2) / n * h_proj/np.mean(h_proj)\n\nMovement definition:\n- Local perturbation: Gershgorin circle method applied to Fourier coefficients\n- Global perturbation: Blend with decaying knowledge base segments\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n\n- Initialization: Random cosine+baseline with frequencies = {1, powers of 1.05 up to 10}\n- Local search: L-BFGS-B for 300s intervals (500 iterations)\n- Global search: Tempered random walk with coefficient space mutations\n- Knowledge base update: Keep top 10 solutions from each resolution\n- Return delta: 0.005 improvement or time limit\n- Projection strategy: Minmax clipping followed by integral adjustment\n\nKey variations [activate only in fallback/constrained mode]:\nA. Linear regression optimization\nB. Incorporate Chebyshev basis (requires separate quadrature)\nC. Add domain-specific random walk (Siamese network-like perturbations)\nD. Use dual frequencies for multi-scale variance\n\n5. Computation Budget and Robustness\n\n- Resolution phases: 300s, 500s, 100s per resolution\n- Coefficients optimization time: 70% total runtime\n- Time checks inserted at solver objectives\n- Graceful failure: Save best candidate if time runs out\n- Fallback condition: Switch to lower frequency dimension or coarser grid\n- Stability: Gradient clipping (eps=1e-12), constraint \u03b4=1e-9\n- Memory: Store last 3-4 valid solutions per resolution\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n\n- Pre-check: Complete integral verification after each projection\n- Post-evaluation: Re-run C_5 on raw output to confirm\n- Feasibility returning condition:\n    np.all((0 <= x <= 1) for x in h)\n    np.isclose(sum(h)*dx, 1, atol=1e-4)\n- Output formatting: Clip to [0,1] after any instability\n- Resolution handling: Prefer 720-point output if provided, else 360/240\n- Sanitary checks:\n    - Monitor ill-conditioned Hessian (ignore if near bounds)\n    - Verify output doesn't violate domain more than 0.5%\n    - Keep base thermalization at 20% until improvement\n\nPersonal Implementation Notes:\n- The projection must handle sum constraint and bounds simultaneously\n- Use np.power(0.99, global_step) for temperature schedule\n- Knowledge_base should persist between function calls\n\nThe algorithm's advantage is systematic exploitation of spectral features without getting stuck in combinatorial noise. It uses derivative information while maintaining global search through Fourier morphing. The fallback includes fall-back representations for broader exploration.",
